From ebf2ecb5484d2d29e8de217f3edf90b4f0b1c666 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 05:28:49 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: add a ptrace-free process debug engine

Stop, inspect, breakpoint and single-step one thread group from the guest
kernel without ptrace, a tracer pid, a signal to the sample or any helper in
the guest. Threads stop the way live process cores do: a task_work callback
parks each thread in a killable sleep at its next return to user mode, with
its registers frozen in its pt_regs; a rendezvous that does not complete in
3 s is reported and the group released, never claimed. Registers and memory
of a parked thread come from the kernel's regset view and access_remote_vm.

A software breakpoint is written only while the group runs and removed at
every stop; the trap is taken by a die notifier, the instruction pointer
rewound and the thread parked, so the sample never sees SIGTRAP. Reads of the
sample's own memory through access_remote_vm (/proc/self/mem) show the
original bytes while a breakpoint is in. Single-step arms EFLAGS.TF on the
parked thread and hides it again in the trap, including in a stepped PUSHF.
Threads created while the group runs are adopted from preallocated records.

debug.c holds the architecture-neutral engine; debug_x86.c the breakpoint
instruction, trap recognition, step arming and the regset list. The control
fence resumes and removes the session before it acknowledges a reattach.
Contract revision stays 17.
